6. IMAP — Internet Message Access Protocol

IMAP is designed to keep email synchronized between the mail server and multiple clients/devices.

Unlike a simple download-and-delete workflow, IMAP can synchronize actions such as:

- Read/unread status
- Moved messages
- Deleted messages
- Mailbox folders

This makes IMAP useful when accessing the same mailbox from multiple devices.

Default Port

IMAP → TCP 143

---

Important IMAP Commands

Command| Purpose
"LOGIN"| Authenticates the user
"SELECT"| Selects a mailbox/folder
"FETCH"| Retrieves information/content from messages
"MOVE"| Moves messages to another mailbox
"COPY"| Copies messages to another mailbox
"LOGOUT"| Ends the IMAP session

FETCH Example

FETCH 3 BODY[]

This requests the content of message number "3".

Another example:

FETCH 4 BODY[]

FETCH → Retrieve message data
4     → Message number
BODY[] → Message body/content

«IMAP commands can have additional syntax and often use command tags such as "A001", "A002", etc., depending on the client/session.»

